;.
  ;@ /flow/definition handbook
  ;= Author and maintain the Shrine concept handbook

    The sections below are editorial briefs, not final handbook prose. Each
    declares its source dependencies. The revision step asks a person or worker
    to synthesize a concept document from captured evidence. Unchanged briefs
    and evidence reuse their completed revisions. Hands retain the drafts,
    frozen evidence, operation traces and guarded publication.

    Each concept owns its persistent questions. Live context lenses follow the
    evidence selected by its brief; attempts retain their frozen readings.
    Missing evidence leaves a reasoned hole, and changed evidence wakes the
    existing native maintenance hand to resume the work.

    ;@ /flow/input sources
    ;= Captured repository evidence

      Bind a complete captured source collection or a live observed feed.

    ;@ /flow/step checked ;/0x11/gov/flows/operations/inventory
    ;@ /flow/bind sources sources
    ;= Check complete source envelopes

    ;@ /flow/step concepts ;/0x11/gov/flows/operations/revise
    ;@ /flow/bind pages document
    ;@ /flow/bind sources checked
    ;= Author or revise the affected concept documents

      Suspend for explicit revision results. Each request has its own native
      Semidoc draft, frozen evidence cells and spun working view. A worker uses
      those captured materials. A person can edit the same draft. A stale worker
      response cannot overwrite that edit or satisfy a changed source dependency.

    ;@ /flow/step handbook_pages ;/0x11/gov/flows/operations/evidence
    ;@ /flow/bind pages concepts
    ;@ /flow/bind sources checked
    ;= Compose the completed concepts and provenance index

      Resume after all required revisions are available. Preserve the written
      concepts as native Semidoc blocks. Source index entries identify evidence;
      they do not reproduce READMEs as chapters.

    ;@ /flow/result handbook_pages
    ;= Publish through the guarded Nenex writer

      An attached hand applies the complete tree when the sources and generated
      destination are still valid. Named notes and cells remain independently
      owned. Conflicting generated prose blocks publication for resolution.

    ;@ /flow/page /
    ;@ /flow/uses nenex flow_harness
    ;= Shrine handbook

      Write a short orientation for someone building native Shrine interfaces.
      Explain the reading path: Concepts, authoring flows, domain trees and
      external formats. Explain that these are maintained concept documents,
      with evidence and editable revision drafts available in flow work. Mention
      where to keep personal notes and the process-local development lifetime.
      Do not document the legacy README-mirroring pipeline as this handbook.

    ;@ /flow/page concepts
    ;@ /flow/uses nenex spun_views
    ;= How the pieces fit

      Give a concise conceptual map: namespace records and typed values; Foil
      programs; Grove contracts; cells and overlays; hands; Goo interaction;
      Semidoc documents. Explain how a document can compose prose, executable
      work and results without requiring a second editor data model. Refer to
      the named concept children in prose; the writer supplies child links.
      Avoid claiming that every intended embedded flow interaction is implemented.

    ;@ /flow/page concepts/namespace_values
    ;@ /flow/uses slots foil_readme native_work
    ;= Records, slots, children and cohesive values

      Teach how to choose a slot, a field in a typed Foil payload, a named child
      record or a reference. Identity, update semantics and independent lifetime
      should drive the choice. Explain why importing external data need not
      create a slot or record for every field. Use an address/contact example
      as a proposed representation; there is no installed address-book schema.

    ;@ /flow/page concepts/foil_reflection
    ;@ /flow/uses foil_readme reflection
    ;= Foil, PLAN and language reflection

      Explain typed functions and retained program structure using the supplied
      reflection demo. Connect inspecting executable definitions to composing
      flows and interpreting captured work. State the limits: reflection alone
      does not discover a reusable procedure or prove dependencies of arbitrary
      work. Do not invent missing manuals or substitute agent self-critique for
      language/PLAN reflection. Include one small supported example if evidence
      allows, explicitly labelled as an example rather than an executed check.

    ;@ /flow/page concepts/grove
    ;@ /flow/uses grove slots
    ;= Grove contracts and native interfaces

      Explain slots, roles, views, panes, selection and publication versus
      installed data. Give a practical authoring sequence for a small domain
      interface. Explain when to reuse existing contracts and when a distinct
      semantic facet needs a declaration. Distinguish matching a role from
      validating an arbitrary write. Do not copy the complete tutorial.

    ;@ /flow/page concepts/cells_overlays
    ;@ /flow/uses spun_views cell_examples native_work
    ;= Cells, bindings and working overlays

      Explain the actual cell and spun contracts, primary tack, secondary input
      views, context and derived results. Contrast the transformations offered
      by dyed, knit, felt and sewn where evidence supports it. Show how a working
      overlay can compose draft document state, evidence and intermediate values.
      Explain the selected live context lens and the separate frozen reading
      retained by an attempt. Show how declared evidence can change without
      rewriting the history of an earlier computation.
      Explain pure reads and why editing a synthesized view needs an explicit
      operation on its owned source. Do not claim a universal write-through
      namespace or virtual execution environment exists.

    ;@ /flow/page concepts/hands
    ;@ /flow/uses hands maintenance_runtime revision_runtime native_work cooperate
    ;= Hands and dependency-driven work

      Explain forms, bowl, crew and reactions in plain language. Describe how a
      hand can notice changed bound evidence, request a revision, wait for a
      response and admit a coherent document update. Explain persistent request
      identity, stale-response checks, pauses, conflicts and failed evidence.
      Explain a question attached to its subject as a persistent shaped hole:
      question, owner, context, current attempt and reason. Explain waiting,
      ready and resolved states, and that unchanged evidence does not replay a
      failed model request. Completed holes can reopen on changed evidence.
      Separate native reactive execution from filesystem/model I/O companions.
      Use this handbook as the worked example; no wall-clock scheduling claim.

    ;@ /flow/page concepts/goo
    ;@ /flow/uses goo goo_readme grove
    ;= Goo as a native interaction contract

      Explain subjects, inputs, selection, controls and declared operations.
      Describe what a headless client can reuse from those declarations and why
      it need not infer each application's behavior from screen coordinates.
      State the actual host/runtime bounds and admission checks. Explain how
      this supports shared human/agent interfaces without claiming that every
      visual affordance or domain operation is already automatable.

    ;@ /flow/page authoring_flows
    ;@ /flow/uses flow_harness cooperate
    ;= Authoring and revising a flow

      Teach flows as reusable methods of work, authored or extracted. Show the
      implemented Semidoc input/step/bind/result/page/uses contract with a minimal
      inert literal example. Explain the document binding as editorial briefs
      when passed to revise, and as fixed prose for deterministic compositions.
      Explain pinned laws, attempts, asynchronous revision results and native
      cells. Clearly identify extraction and general branching as future work.
      Explain that a provider can return empty source with a missing-evidence
      note, retaining work rather than fabricating a completed concept. Show
      where to inspect its hole, current attempt and live context in Nenex.
      Explain how to reuse this machinery for an onboarding guide or a research
      synthesis by changing briefs, source dependencies and composed operations.
      Teach the Nenex Page tools workflow: Author a new flow, Edit source and
      Save page, Publish flow, bind inputs and Attach maintenance. Explain that
      saving source alone does not publish or replace a running definition.

    ;@ /flow/page domain_trees
    ;@ /flow/uses nenex slots native_work
    ;= Structuring a tree for a domain

      Give actionable rules for containment, references, cohesive typed data,
      identity and independent work. Include research and contact-directory
      examples. Explain numbered Semidoc content versus named documents, work,
      cells and inputs. Say when to author a reusable flow and when a simple
      calculation or live view suffices. Avoid a universal ontology.

    ;@ /flow/page external_formats
    ;@ /flow/uses slots flow_harness native_work spun_views
    ;= Loading external information into Shrine

      Explain capture, decode, normalize, project and admit as distinct
      responsibilities. Preserve source identity and missing values. Explain
      bounded collections and native projections. Use a hypothetical address
      import to illustrate the boundaries. Discuss when fields belong together in a Foil payload rather
      than proliferating slots or children. Link conceptually to the address/OSM
      child. The old address-book and Austin demo have been removed; do not
      describe an installed importer or imply that this flow reads OSM.

    ;@ /flow/page external_formats/address_osm
    ;@ /flow/uses slots foil_readme spun_views
    ;= Address decomposition and an OSM representation policy

      Present a clearly labelled proposed address/OSM import policy. Preserve
      source identity, original fields and structural references; derive an
      address facet. Keep related fields in a cohesive Foil value, and create
      independently addressable entities only where reference or lifecycle
      requires them. Keep number, unit and postal code textual. Explain how
      overlays can project the cohesive value into useful interfaces. No OSM
      importer or address-book application is installed by this handbook.
      Do not claim street normalization proves geographic connectivity.

    ;@ /flow/page maintenance
    ;@ /flow/uses flow_harness maintenance_runtime revision_runtime native_work codex_work
    ;= Maintaining this handbook

      Explain the running lifecycle using Nenex Page tools: publishing a saved
      definition, binding sources, selecting a native provider and attaching
      maintenance. Show how to inspect drafts, evidence and working overlays,
      and pause or resume from the reader. Explain that repository evidence uses
      an ordinary filesystem capture instance and a native capture hand; evidence
      updates do not recompile the harness. There is no standalone Python observer
      or work scheduler. Explain how the same Cells surface in Nenex exposes
      maintenance status, drafts, evidence and holes, and how Accept saved draft
      uses guarded native admission.
      Explain source-scoped invalidation, frozen revision evidence, publication
      only after all required concepts are ready, no-op inputs and protecting
      edited generated prose. Say model output is checked for native structure
      and freshness, not proven factually correct. Explain manual completion
      uses the same native operation and that no model credentials are stored
      in a flow. Keep instructions concise and consistent with the captured code.
